# Build-context hygiene for both images. Everything listed here is regenerated,
# machine-local, or must never reach an image.

# Version control and tooling caches
.git/
.gitignore
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Machine-local interpreter: a Windows .venv copied into a Linux image is
# ~200 MB of unusable binaries and the usual cause of "works on my machine".
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Raw survey data. Never ships, by design: the handover excludes raw survey
# rows, household ids and coordinates (README, final line). config/paths.py
# would put Stage 0 input here.
data/

# The Next.js workspace builds in its own image from this same directory, so its
# sources have to stay in context. Its machine-local install, build output and
# generated data do not: frontend/data is rebuilt from backend/artifacts by the
# frontend build, and frontend/node_modules is the wrong platform and by far the
# biggest thing here. The frontend image copies only its app, approved data,
# and documentation.
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/.next/
frontend/data/
frontend/.env.local
frontend/next-env.d.ts
frontend/*.tsbuildinfo

# Logs, editors, OS droppings
*.log
.streamlit/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Docker's own files. Not excluded: frontend/Dockerfile has to stay in the context it
# builds from, and whether a bare `Dockerfile` pattern would also match
# frontend/Dockerfile is a matcher detail worth two kilobytes to not depend on.
